 "productionNotes": "No single cumulative total is asserted here, because the two most authoritative published figures do not agree and the difference is systematic rather than a rounding artefact. Chris Rees's Complete Classic Mini 1959-2000 carries a year-by-year, model-by-model breakdown supplied by the British Motor Industry Heritage Trust that totals 5,378,776 cars. Jon Pressnell's Mini - The Definitive History carries a comparable breakdown supplied by MG Rover that totals 5,387,862. AROnline's line-by-line comparison of the two tables finds them broadly in step until 1977 and diverging in every year afterwards, by as much as 1,811 cars one way in 1977 and 2,590 the other way in 1987 - odd, given that assembly was by then largely concentrated at Longbridge. Neither camp explains the other. Both tables count saloons of all seven Marks, the Clubman and 1275 GT, the estates, the vans and pick-ups, the Riley Elf and Wolseley Hornet, and the Moke including Australian and Portuguese output. Neither captures the full extent of licensed and knocked-down assembly overseas: Craig Watson, writing in the Australian magazine The Mini Experience, added that work and arrived at at least 6,302,000 Minis, which is a different question answered with a wider boundary rather than a contradiction of the other two. The round '5.3 million' repeated by BMW and by most of the press sits inside all of this and settles nothing. The cutoff matters as much as the count: figures quoted for 1959-2000 are not comparable with figures that stop at the end of Longbridge saloon output or that exclude commercials. Cumulative milestones are better established - one million by 1965, two million by 1969, three million by 1972, four million by 1976 and five million by 1986 - and the last car left Longbridge on 4 October 2000.",
